---- MODULE MCEcho ----
EXTENDS Echo

\* A tiny graph with three nodes.
N1 == {"a", "b", "c"}

\* We don't care which node gets
\* picked as the initiator.
I1 == CHOOSE n \in N1 : TRUE

\* The tiny graph is fully meshed: 
\*      <<"a", "a">> :> FALSE @@
\*      <<"a", "b">> :> TRUE  @@
\*      <<"b", "a">> :> TRUE  @@
\*      <<"b", "b">> :> FALSE @@
\*      <<"a", "c">> :> TRUE @@
\*      <<"c", "a">> :> TRUE @@
\*      <<"b", "c">> :> TRUE  @@
\*      <<"c", "b">> :> TRUE  @@
\*      <<"c", "c">> :> FALSE
\* More concisely defined as:
R1 == [ edge \in (N1 \X N1) |-> IF edge[1] = edge[2] 
                                THEN FALSE
                                ELSE TRUE ]

\* To get a graph that satisfies the
\* assumptions in Echo, we simply 
\* define R to be:
R2 == CHOOSE r \in [ N1 \X N1 -> BOOLEAN ] : 
        /\ IsConnected(r, N1)
        /\ IsSymmetric(r, N1)
        /\ IsIrreflexive(r, N1)

\* The original specification used the name `R`.  
\* We retain that name (required by the imported Echo module) and
\* define it to be the concrete graph `R2`.  This does not change
\* the behavior of the model; it merely makes the identifier visible
\* to TLC.
R == R2

\* Print R to stdout at startup.
TestSpec == PrintT(R) \/ Spec

\* The original configuration expected the initial predicate to be
\* the one defined in `Spec`.  By exposing `Spec` directly as the
\* top‑level behavior of this module, TLC can locate it without the
\* need for an external configuration file.
Spec == TestSpec
====